# DecentAI's configuration — one file for the whole compose stack.
#
# On your own computer you do not fill this in:
#
#   python bootstrap/setup.py
#
# writes config/deploy.env from it, with every change-me replaced by a value
# it generated, and starts the stack. As written here the file is for
# that: one person's own computer, plain HTTP, this machine only.
#
# For a server, copy it to config/deploy.env, replace every change-me, set
# what the "On a server" notes below say, and start it as
# docs/run/deploying.md says.
#
# The Data Core and the seeder are handed this file whole. The runtime is
# handed only the settings docker-compose.yml names for it, and the
# Execution Sandbox none of the platform's: a new setting for the
# runtime is named there as well as set here.
#
# Four generated values, four generators (setup.py runs them for you):
#   python bootstrap/generate_service_keys.py    → the Data Core's RS256 pair
#   python bootstrap/generate_node_authority.py  → the install's node authority
#   python bootstrap/generate_secret_keys.py     → the AES-256-GCM secret keys
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#                                                → TOKEN_SECRET_KEY
# and one optional, for notifications on phones and computers:
#   python bootstrap/generate_vapid_keys.py      → the VAPID pair

# ── What the stack is made of ────────────────────────────────────────
# The Compose files, separated by colons: docker-compose.yml and what
# is laid over it. Compose reads this from the file itself, in every
# `docker compose --env-file config/deploy.env` command and in
# setup.py's, so no command names them. A second Execution Sandbox on
# this computer: docker-compose.yml:compose/sandboxes.yml. A server:
# docker-compose.yml:compose/server.yml (docs/run/deploying.md). A
# container of a file taken out of the
# list is removed the next time setup.py starts the stack.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml
# The colon above, on every system: Windows' own is a semicolon.
COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:

# ── Where the engine is reached ──────────────────────────────────────
# The engine is a headless API: no page, no TLS and no proxy of its
# own. The app is the web client (decentai-web-client), served on its
# own — its container, a static host — and told this engine's address;
# on a server, TLS and the public hostname are a terminator of your
# choosing in front of the Data Core's port (docs/run/deploying.md).
# The engine and the app are different origins, and this section names
# both.
#
# The Data Core's port on this computer, and who may reach it: this
# computer alone. On a server: the port the TLS terminator forwards
# to, and LISTEN=0.0.0.0 where the terminator is on another host
# (compose/server.yml does that).
PORT=4280
LISTEN=127.0.0.1
# The engine's own address, as browsers and OAuth providers reach it:
# the OAuth return address and a webhook's address are made from it.
# On a server: https:// and the engine's domain, as the terminator
# answers it.
PUBLIC_ENGINE_URL=http://localhost:4280
# ── The web client ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every origin the web client is served from, comma-separated: the
# browser calls this engine from there, with the session token, and
# the Data Core answers no other origin. On this computer: the web
# client's container (docker compose up in decentai-web-client, port
# 4300) and its development server (npm start, port 4200). On a
# server: https:// and the app's domain. Nothing else goes here — a
# wildcard would let any page act for a signed-in person.
CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4300,http://localhost:4200
# Where people open the app, exactly: links in invitations, password
# resets and notifications are made from it, and the OAuth popup
# reports to this origin alone. One of the origins above.
PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:4300
# Where OAuth providers send the browser back after consent. Leave empty
# to use PUBLIC_ENGINE_URL/oauth/callback, the engine's own route;
# register that exact address with every provider app.
OAUTH_REDIRECT_URL=

# ── MongoDB ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The URI carries the same credentials — keep the three lines in sync.
MONGO_ROOT_USERNAME=decentai
MONGO_ROOT_PASSWORD=change-me
MONGO_URI=mongodb://decentai:change-me@mongo:27017/?authSource=admin
MONGO_DATABASE_NAME=decentai

# ── Service topology (compose service names — leave as is) ───────────
# How an execution node reaches the Data Core: its gateway (a chat's
# contract, state, messages, packages) and its node link. The Data
# Core is given no node's address: nodes connect to it. The Compose
# service name here; 127.0.0.1 where both run in one container group.
DATA_CORE_INTERNAL_URL=http://data-core:8000
# Where agents installed from a repository are kept (a volume, so an
# approved agent survives a restart).
AI_RUNTIME_AGENTS_INSTALL_DIR=/data/agents
# The port of the runtime's proxy, which is an agent's only way out:
# the Execution Sandbox points every worker at it. Unset is 8002; a
# number here is read by both containers.
# AI_RUNTIME_EGRESS_PORT=8002
# What the agents are given together, in their own container: memory
# (2g, 1500m) and processors (2, 1.5). Unset is no limit. An agent that
# takes more memory than this is ended, and the chat says which.
# SANDBOX_MEMORY=2g
# SANDBOX_CPUS=2
# What each Execution Sandbox says of itself, comma-separated; the node
# reports them with its sandboxes. SANDBOX_2_LABELS is for the second
# sandbox (compose/sandboxes.yml).
# SANDBOX_LABELS=gpu
# SANDBOX_2_LABELS=
# Whose word the Data Core takes for a client's address (the
# X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto headers): the TLS terminator in
# front of it, by its address or range as the Data Core sees it,
# comma-separated. Anybody can send those headers; believing them from
# the wrong peer lets a client dodge the sign-in lockout by address and
# write any address into a session's record. "*" believes every peer:
# right on a computer of one's own, where the port is reached from this
# machine alone, and on a server only where the Data Core's port is
# reached through the terminator and nothing else. Unset, nobody's word
# is taken: behind a terminator every client is then the terminator,
# and the lockout counts everybody as one.
FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=*
# The platform's own speech models, in a container beside it. As the
# stack first starts it downloads the one that writes a spoken message
# down (about 490 MB, once). Set and empty, it downloads nothing until
# an administrator chooses it in the app; "transcription,speech"
# fetches the voices that say replies aloud as well.
# SPEECH_FETCH=

# ── First run ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A deployment nobody is in yet opens on a first-run page: the person
# there names the organization and becomes its administrator, and the
# page is gone for good. It asks for a setup code first. Empty: the
# deployment makes one and writes it to its log (`docker compose
# --env-file config/deploy.env logs init`). "none": nothing is asked - only for
# an address no other computer reaches; setup.py writes it. Anything
# else is the code.
SETUP_CODE=
#
# Or skip the page: name the first administrator here and the seeder
# makes them (an automated deployment). Left empty, the page does it.
# Named, they are also the way back in for a deployment where nobody
# administers any more.
ORG_NAME=
ADMIN_NAME=
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Organizations from the sign-in page ──────────────────────────────
# "open": anybody may create an organization of their own from the
# sign-in page, and be its administrator, once a link sent to their
# address is followed - so it opens only where outbound email is set
# up, below. Each organization is apart from every other, and needs its
# own language-model keys. "closed" (the default): organizations are
# made on the machine, with bootstrap/organizations.py. Joining one that
# exists is by invitation either way.
ORG_SIGNUP=closed

# ── Authentication ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
TOKEN_SECRET_KEY=change-me
# A cookie marked for HTTPS alone is never sent back over plain HTTP.
# On a server, where the address is https: true.
JWT_COOKIE_SECURE=false
JWT_COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax

# ── The Data Core's key (bootstrap/generate_service_keys.py) ─────────
# One line each, exactly as printed (\n escapes preserved). The Data
# Core signs everything it tells an execution node with the private
# half; every node holds the public half and believes nothing else.
DATA_CORE_SERVICE_PRIVATE_KEY=change-me
DATA_CORE_SERVICE_PUBLIC_KEY=change-me

# ── Execution nodes (bootstrap/generate_node_authority.py) ───────────
# Nodes are admitted by authorities, never one by one
# (docs/system/execution-nodes.md). This install's own authority: the
# seeder registers this public half as a shared authority. Its private
# half is NOT here: it is in config/node-authority.env, given to node-issuer
# alone, which signs the bundled node's credentials — the Data Core,
# the seeder and the node never hold it. One line, as printed.
NODE_AUTHORITY_PUBLIC_KEY=change-me
# The name the bundled node's credential gives it.
# NODE_NAME=bundled
# Whose administrators are this deployment's operator, who registers
# and revokes shared authorities. Empty: the first organization.
# OPERATOR_ORG_ID=

# ── The execution node ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# What the AI Runtime and its Execution Sandboxes prove to each other,
# AI_RUNTIME_SANDBOX_SECRET, is NOT here: it is in config/sandbox.env, given
# to the runtime and its sandboxes alone (config/sandbox.env.example).

# ── Encrypted data (bootstrap/generate_secret_keys.py) ───────────────
SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=01:change-me
SECRET_ENCRYPTION_ACTIVE=01

# ── Outbound email (invitations, password resets) ────────────────────
# Unset, nothing is sent: the Users page adds a person with a temporary
# password shown once instead of inviting them, "Forgot password" says
# how a password is reset on the machine, and notifications reach
# browsers only. That keeps a computer of one's own usable without a
# mail provider - and means a server that forgets this never delivers.
#
# Any provider's SMTP server. SMTP_HOST and MAIL_FROM are the switch:
# without both nothing is sent. SMTP_SECURITY is starttls (port 587),
# ssl (port 465) or none; MAIL_FROM must be an address the server lets
# you send as.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
SMTP_USERNAME=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=

# ── Notifications (bootstrap/generate_vapid_keys.py) ─────────────────
# Web push to the browsers and installed apps a person subscribed. Empty,
# nothing is pushed, and a person is told by email where email is set up.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com
# Phones running the DecentAI app are pushed to through Expo's service,
# which needs nothing here. An Expo access token only raises its limits.
EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── File storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where uploaded files and approved agent packages are kept: local (the
# volumes), s3, azure_blob or gcs. Each cloud signs in as the deployment
# first — an AWS role, an Azure managed identity, a Google service
# account — and the keys below are for a deployment that has none.
# Moving an existing deployment: bootstrap/move_storage.py.
FILE_STORAGE_PROVIDER=local
# Optional, for every cloud: a folder inside the bucket or container.
# STORAGE_PREFIX=decentai
# Amazon S3, or a store that speaks its protocol (MinIO, R2: set the endpoint).
# STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=
# STORAGE_S3_REGION=
# STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=
# STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# STORAGE_S3_KMS_KEY_ID=
# Azure Blob Storage: the account's URL, signed in as the deployment, or a
# connection string.
# STORAGE_AZURE_CONTAINER=
# STORAGE_AZURE_ACCOUNT_URL=https://<account>.blob.core.windows.net
# STORAGE_AZURE_CONNECTION_STRING=
# Google Cloud Storage: application default credentials, or the file
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS names inside the container.
# STORAGE_GCS_BUCKET=
# STORAGE_GCS_PROJECT=
# STORAGE_GCS_ENDPOINT=

# A repository the Agents page may suggest as a source, with one click.
# Empty by default on purpose — a platform that shipped pointing at
# somebody else's repository would be choosing that for every deployment.
# The project's own agents are at https://github.com/decentai-io/decentai-agents.git;
# that address, or a fork's, is a sensible value.
REFERENCE_CATALOG_URL=

